#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770} :activity/id #uuid "9b6ebd1c-f083-5c13-ba2f-eefcd05878c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162 :topic/id #uuid "df4f6208-5d6c-5dc9-bd38-c7d85e32fb73" :topic/math-academy-id 239} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060969 :tutorial/content "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbd2ff75-3253-5df6-ac32-55d5e3a95cf5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6750 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "53a0851d-e1e9-5f13-a1f9-1e0ad720c677" :step/math-academy-id 40878 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f45f69-d8e7-5e79-a1ec-38d8912cbc19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483411}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "6325c0ca-8510-5c9d-8b87-b9042fe377ed" :step/math-academy-id 40879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060970 :tutorial/content "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." :tutorial/id #uuid "633202af-a5bd-58ac-a806-89458305a7a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6751 :tutorial/title "Half-Open Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "149280d9-7b92-52c0-ab69-a011aca574b3" :step/math-academy-id 40882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdf77469-6c2a-5339-b216-3bc5e2316e4d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483383}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "9d9e1551-d8ec-536f-9036-9555d43eae22" :step/math-academy-id 40883 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c3e092-d9ff-5dc9-ad1e-f31aa2b226ba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483422}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "6f9b14a3-07a8-5f6b-9c69-4df4a979ee4a" :step/math-academy-id 40887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f519fa9-de89-5b00-aa02-312cf8e32cba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483384}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "082973dc-66d1-5d9c-9dc3-ae132f451431" :step/math-academy-id 40993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060973 :tutorial/content "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "27f305da-f09b-53e5-a8b8-a30d29eb0f15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6821 :tutorial/title "Closed Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "b373d760-0d5d-5ec6-a830-9c562d00e5be" :step/math-academy-id 41408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771}}] :activity/title "Interval Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059917}]}
